      "dom_id": "question-47627",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y={log}_{3}⁡x?$\n\nI. The domain is $x>0$\n\nII. Its vertical asymptote is $x=0$\n\nIII. It passes through the point $(3,2)$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's plot the graph of $y={log}_{3}⁡x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14257461/assets/2030ca3ff094999814610c1533af1fea37315d49d8d6c62635bc30b5c16d54c4.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph we can see that the function is defined for $x>0.$\n\n- Statement II is true.\n\n- Statement III is false. Substituting $x=3$ into the equation of the curve gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={log}_{3}⁡(3) \\\\  & ={log}_{3}⁡({3}^{1}) \\\\  & =1\\ne 2\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and II are true.",
